WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.478
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Proficiency.

2
00:00:05.778 --> 00:00:08.828
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.128 --> 00:00:16.540
Unit four: The Ethics of Wealth. In this unit, you will debate the moral responsibilities that come with wealth.

4
00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:19.178
effective altruism.

5
00:00:19.878 --> 00:00:24.896
effective altruism means a movement using evidence to do the most good with resources.

6
00:00:25.196 --> 00:00:29.355
Effective altruism asks which charities save the most lives.

7
00:00:30.055 --> 00:00:33.845
Your turn. Say: effective altruism.

8
00:00:36.545 --> 00:00:38.301
effective altruism.

9
00:00:39.317 --> 00:00:41.049
philanthropic foundation.

10
00:00:41.749 --> 00:00:47.069
philanthropic foundation means an organisation set up to give money to good causes.

11
00:00:47.369 --> 00:00:50.727
The philanthropic foundation funds malaria research.

12
00:00:51.427 --> 00:00:54.997
Your turn. Say: philanthropic foundation.

13
00:00:57.697 --> 00:00:59.452
philanthropic foundation.

14
00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:01.718
inherited wealth.

15
00:01:02.418 --> 00:01:07.250
inherited wealth means money and property received from family.

16
00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:10.142
Inherited wealth can entrench inequality.

17
00:01:10.842 --> 00:01:14.377
Your turn. Say: inherited wealth.

18
00:01:17.077 --> 00:01:18.136
inherited wealth.

19
00:01:19.150 --> 00:01:20.952
conspicuous consumption.

20
00:01:21.652 --> 00:01:25.916
conspicuous consumption means spending to display wealth and status.

21
00:01:26.216 --> 00:01:29.899
Luxury cars are a classic form of conspicuous consumption.

22
00:01:30.599 --> 00:01:34.226
Your turn. Say: conspicuous consumption.

23
00:01:36.926 --> 00:01:38.636
conspicuous consumption.

24
00:01:39.650 --> 00:01:41.162
moral obligation.

25
00:01:41.862 --> 00:01:46.369
moral obligation means a duty based on ethics rather than law.

26
00:01:46.669 --> 00:01:49.365
Do the rich have a moral obligation to give?

27
00:01:50.065 --> 00:01:53.217
Your turn. Say: moral obligation.

28
00:01:55.917 --> 00:01:57.406
moral obligation.

29
00:01:58.417 --> 00:02:00.312
utilitarian calculus.

30
00:02:01.012 --> 00:02:06.100
utilitarian calculus means weighing actions by the total happiness they produce.

31
00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:10.396
A utilitarian calculus might favour cheap, life-saving aid.

32
00:02:11.096 --> 00:02:15.188
Your turn. Say: utilitarian calculus.

33
00:02:17.888 --> 00:02:19.888
utilitarian calculus.

34
00:02:20.883 --> 00:02:22.140
tax haven.

35
00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:27.962
tax haven means a country with very low taxes used to avoid tax elsewhere.

36
00:02:28.262 --> 00:02:30.645
Billions are hidden in tax havens.

37
00:02:31.345 --> 00:02:34.485
Your turn. Say: tax haven.

38
00:02:37.185 --> 00:02:38.209
tax haven.

39
00:02:39.217 --> 00:02:40.415
wealth tax.

40
00:02:41.115 --> 00:02:45.704
wealth tax means a tax on the total value of a person's assets.

41
00:02:46.004 --> 00:02:48.526
Several countries have debated a wealth tax.

42
00:02:49.226 --> 00:02:52.099
Your turn. Say: wealth tax.

43
00:02:54.799 --> 00:02:55.939
wealth tax.

44
00:02:56.950 --> 00:02:59.159
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:59.459 --> 00:03:02.921
Ms. Sarah, does great wealth bring great moral obligation?

46
00:03:04.217 --> 00:03:10.294
Many philosophers think so. Peter Singer, for one, argues that we must give far more than we do.

47
00:03:11.583 --> 00:03:16.105
That's effective altruism, isn't it? Using evidence to maximise good.

48
00:03:17.417 --> 00:03:22.426
Yes. This approach relies on a kind of utilitarian calculus.

49
00:03:23.717 --> 00:03:28.482
Critics say it ignores justice. Such charity can't replace fair taxation.

50
00:03:29.783 --> 00:03:34.247
That point is well made. Some propose a wealth tax for that very reason.

51
00:03:35.550 --> 00:03:38.037
Meanwhile, money flows to tax havens.

52
00:03:39.350 --> 00:03:44.333
Which suggests that the former problem, giving, may matter less than the latter, avoidance.

53
00:03:45.617 --> 00:03:48.708
Grammar focus: Cohesion and reference.

54
00:03:49.008 --> 00:03:52.458
Cohesive reference connects ideas across sentences.

55
00:03:52.758 --> 00:03:58.789
This approach relies on a utilitarian calculus. Such charity can't replace fair taxation.

56
00:03:59.089 --> 00:04:01.681
The former problem may matter less than the latter.

57
00:04:01.981 --> 00:04:08.984
Summarising nouns (this approach, such a view) link ideas smoothly and avoid repetition in long arguments.

58
00:04:09.983 --> 00:04:13.077
Now read and listen. How Much Should the Rich Give?.

59
00:04:13.377 --> 00:04:18.604
Wealth is distributed with remarkable inequality across the globe.

60
00:04:19.917 --> 00:04:25.748
Philosopher Peter Singer argues that, if we can prevent suffering at little cost, we ought to do so.

61
00:04:27.050 --> 00:04:34.588
This argument underpins effective altruism, a movement that evaluates charities by measurable impact.

62
00:04:35.883 --> 00:04:41.505
Such an approach has directed millions towards cheap, life-saving interventions.

63
00:04:42.817 --> 00:04:50.192
Critics, however, contend that philanthropy allows the wealthy to choose causes without democratic accountability.

64
00:04:51.483 --> 00:04:59.230
They argue that fair taxation, including measures against tax havens, matters more than voluntary generosity.

65
00:05:00.517 --> 00:05:06.684
Conspicuous consumption, meanwhile, raises questions about the moral meaning of luxury.

66
00:05:07.983 --> 00:05:12.781
The former debate concerns how to give; the latter, whether giving is enough.

67
00:05:14.083 --> 00:05:16.921
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:17.221 --> 00:05:20.010
Spending to display wealth and status is blank.

69
00:05:20.310 --> 00:05:26.541
A, conspicuous consumption. B, effective altruism. C, wealth tax.

70
00:05:30.550 --> 00:05:33.908
The answer is A: conspicuous consumption.

71
00:05:35.417 --> 00:05:39.785
He studied law and medicine; blank led him to research.

72
00:05:40.085 --> 00:05:44.307
A, the latter. B, the after. C, the second one.

73
00:05:48.317 --> 00:05:51.257
The answer is A: the latter.

74
00:05:52.750 --> 00:05:55.655
blank an approach has saved many lives.

75
00:05:55.955 --> 00:05:59.481
A, Such. B, So. C, This such.

76
00:06:03.483 --> 00:06:05.692
The answer is A: Such.

77
00:06:07.183 --> 00:06:11.168
A country with very low taxes used for avoidance is a blank.

78
00:06:11.468 --> 00:06:18.012
A, tax haven. B, philanthropic foundation. C, mandate.

79
00:06:22.017 --> 00:06:24.492
The answer is A: tax haven.

80
00:06:25.983 --> 00:06:30.293
According to the text, what do critics say philanthropy lacks?

81
00:06:30.593 --> 00:06:35.245
A, money. B, democratic accountability. C, evidence.

82
00:06:39.250 --> 00:06:42.736
The answer is B: democratic accountability.

83
00:06:44.250 --> 00:06:50.861
Now it's your turn. Part one: Do wealthy people have a moral duty to give away much of their wealth?

84
00:06:56.161 --> 00:07:01.551
Part two: Use cohesive reference to compare two positions and conclude.

85
00:07:07.851 --> 00:07:22.244
Singer's view is that we must give whenever we can prevent suffering cheaply. The opposing view prioritises fair taxation over charity. Of the two, the latter seems more just, though the former still has moral force for individuals.

86
00:07:22.544 --> 00:07:25.391
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:26.383 --> 00:07:30.998
Great work today! You learned eight new words and cohesion and reference.

88
00:07:31.298 --> 00:07:34.554
Today's value is kindness. Be kind to everyone.

89
00:07:34.854 --> 00:07:37.460
See you in Unit five. Goodbye!

90
00:07:37.760 --> 00:07:40.273
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

